7
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:34,680
We've already examined chromatography as an efficient process for separating components

8
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:38,400
of a mixture on the basis of differing polarity.

9
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:43,440
This occurs due to different degrees of interaction with the stationary phase, as components are

10
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:45,880
pushed along with a mobile phase.

11
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:50,280
But for TLC, we are only able to separate the tiny bit of the mixture that is spotted

12
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:51,520
onto the plate.

13
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:55,600
We will often want to perform chromatography on large samples.

14
00:00:55,600 --> 00:01:01,800
For this we will need column chromatography, a commonly used technique in chemistry, biology,

15
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:06,080
and industry, which we have covered from a conceptual standpoint over in the lecture-based

16
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:10,440
series, so be sure to review that video for additional context.

17
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:15,440
To perform column chromatography, we will need a column, that's this.

18
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:18,240
This will be loaded with the stationary phase.

19
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:23,000
The mobile phase is a solvent solution that moves down through the column along with

20
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,440
your sample, just as solvent moved up the plate in TLC.

21
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:32,200
You can then collect fractions as they come out of the column, using a series of small

22
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:37,000
flasks, such that the components of the mixture will arrive in different fractions

23
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,680
due to their differing rates of travel down the column, according to how well they interact

24
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:43,480
with the stationary phase.

25
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:50,580
The most common type of column chromatography in a chemistry lab is silica gel column chromatography.

26
00:01:50,580 --> 00:01:55,680
Solvent is added to the gel to form a slurry, and this slurry of silica gel acts as the

27
00:01:55,680 --> 00:02:00,360
stationary phase, while the solvent system that passes through the column acts as the

28
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:01,880
mobile phase.

29
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:06,640
The stationary phase can separate compounds based on their differences in polarity precisely

30
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:12,200
the same way as the TLC plate, only now moving through a 3D matrix rather than along

31
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:14,020
a 2D surface.

32
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:17,000
The key concept is still adsorption.

33
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,940
The materials being separated are attracted to the silica beads to differing degrees depending

34
00:02:21,940 --> 00:02:26,880
on their polarity, because of all the hydroxyl groups protruding from their surfaces, which

35
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:32,360
can make hydrogen bonds or other types of dipole-dipole interactions with polar molecules,

36
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:35,840
which will cause them to move more slowly through the column.

37
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:39,960
Less polar molecules will move more quickly since they won't interact as well with

38
00:02:39,960 --> 00:02:44,640
the stationary phase and are thus pushed along with the solvent.

39
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:46,400
Now it's time to perform the technique.

40
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:49,260
We'll start by packing our glass column.

41
00:02:49,260 --> 00:02:53,640
As you can see, the column has a stopcock at the bottom, so make sure it's closed.

42
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:56,800
We'll then add a cotton ball to the bottom.

43
00:02:56,800 --> 00:03:01,280
This is so that our gel doesn't go through, as it is a fine powder and can easily slip

44
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:04,020
through when we open the stopcock.

45
00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:07,920
Using a glass rod like this one, we'll push the cotton down like this.

46
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:12,840
Let's then secure our column to a ring stand and clamps.

47
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:17,840
Next we will add a fine layer of sand so that it fills the curved bottom section and forms

48
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:20,960
a level surface on top of the cotton ball.

49
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:25,320
Add some solvent to clean the sand off the sides by using a pasture pipette.

50
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:29,740
We want all the sand to go to the bottom and not mix with our silica.

51
00:03:29,740 --> 00:03:33,320
Make sure the sand level is as horizontal as possible.

52
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:38,240
If it's a little slanted, you can just gently tap the side of the column like this.

53
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:43,280
Now we're going to prepare the gel by adding 25 milliliters of our solvent to the silica

54
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:44,340
powder.

55
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:49,360
The solvent we're using is also known as the eluent for column chromatography.

56
00:03:49,360 --> 00:03:55,040
An appropriate solvent for column chromatography can be chosen by performing TLC on your mixture

57
00:03:55,040 --> 00:04:00,600
in various solvents and combinations, such as hexanes and ethyl acetate.

58
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:04,300
You can then choose one that gives the best separation.

59
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:10,060
Since the TLC plate also utilizes silica, just like in our column, the separation pattern

60
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:12,180
will therefore be the same.

61
00:04:12,180 --> 00:04:17,380
In today's experiment we're using a 1 to 1 acetone ethanol mixture as our solvent.

62
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:19,940
Let's add it to the silica powder.

63
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:24,240
The size of your column and volume of your mixture will determine how much silica and

64
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:25,940
solvent you use.

65
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:30,240
You need a level tall enough to allow complete separation of your samples, and you should

66
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:33,360
aim for this filling up about two thirds of the column.

67
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:38,120
Let's stir the mixture with our rod until we get a nice slurry.

68
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:42,240
After we've made a slurry of our silica, we'll add it to our column by using a glass

69
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:43,240
funnel.

70
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:50,420
You want to keep mixing with a stir rod right up until you pour it in quickly like this.

71
00:04:50,420 --> 00:04:54,440
Use more solvent to remove the rest of the silica from the beaker, and also to wash

72
00:04:54,440 --> 00:04:57,060
any gel off the sides of the column.

73
00:04:57,060 --> 00:04:59,700
For reference, this is called wet loading.

74
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:04,700
An alternative is dry loading, which consists of adding the dry powder to the column, and

75
00:05:04,700 --> 00:05:06,800
then the solvent after.

76
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:11,080
This technique can cause a lot of bubbles that interfere with the separation, and tends

77
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:14,920
to be less effective, so wet loading is suggested.

78
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:19,120
As you add the gel to your column, be sure to tap on the column gently to make sure

79
00:05:19,120 --> 00:05:23,940
it stays flat on top, and to get rid of any bubbles or uneven packing.

80
00:05:23,940 --> 00:05:27,640
After we've packed our column, we'll open the stopcock to remove some of the

81
00:05:27,640 --> 00:05:28,640
solvent.

82
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:32,640
For chromatography, it is important that you don't let your column run dry, meaning

83
00:05:32,640 --> 00:05:36,280
all of the gel must always be submerged in solvent.

84
00:05:36,280 --> 00:05:41,540
In other words, the solvent line must always remain above the stationary phase.

85
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:45,080
As you progress with the technique, you will always keep an eye on the solvent line

86
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:48,720
and add more solvent when necessary to keep the column wet.

87
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:54,240
If it is not wet, it can cause cracking of the silica, which will cause unreliable separation.

88
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:57,720
For right now, let's allow the solvent line to go down until it's above the

89
00:05:57,720 --> 00:05:59,840
silica gel line like this.

90
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:03,280
We're now ready to load the sample.

91
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:07,880
Today we're separating a mixture of methyl red and bromocresyl green.

92
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:12,440
You may know them as indicators that are used in titration, but if you look at their structures,

93
00:06:12,440 --> 00:06:16,060
you can see that they have different functional groups, which allows them to be separated

94
00:06:16,060 --> 00:06:17,920
based on their polarities.

95
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:22,080
You can see that bromocresyl green has many polar groups, and that makes it more

96
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:24,180
polar than methyl red.

97
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:28,620
We are using these chemicals today because they are brightly colored, and we can therefore

98
00:06:28,620 --> 00:06:33,620
visualize them as they move through the column, which will help us understand what is happening.

99
00:06:33,620 --> 00:06:35,860
This will usually not be the case.

100
00:06:35,860 --> 00:06:39,360
Typically we can't see compounds as they move through the column, which makes things

101
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:40,860
more challenging.

102
00:06:40,860 --> 00:06:44,920
When adding your sample to the column, if you have isolated the organic products from

103
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:49,980
a chemical reaction to form an oil, we want to dissolve that oil in as small an amount

104
00:06:49,980 --> 00:06:51,920
of our solvent as possible.

105
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:57,040
This will allow us to load the sample as a thin band, which will result in good separation.

106
00:06:57,040 --> 00:07:01,060
If we use too much solvent, the compounds will be very spread out, and it will be harder

107
00:07:01,060 --> 00:07:02,320
to separate them.

108
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:05,300
Today, our mixture is already ready to load.

109
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:07,780
To load the sample, we have to be careful.

110
00:07:07,780 --> 00:07:11,640
We usually take it into a glass pipette, and then lower the pipette as close to the

111
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:17,640
top of the column as possible, slowly letting it out to make sure that our layer is even.

112
00:07:17,640 --> 00:07:21,360
We have to be gentle because we don't want to disturb the silica layer.

113
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:25,960
In this case, we're adding a quarter of a milliliter of a mixture of our two samples,

114
00:07:25,960 --> 00:07:30,660
and we can allow it to run down part of the side of the column to load gently.

115
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:35,160
After loading the sample and verifying that all of it has been adsorbed onto the column,

116
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:38,480
we can add another layer of sand as a protective layer.

117
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:40,620
Let's add about a centimeter.

118
00:07:40,620 --> 00:07:43,360
We can now fill up the column with our eluent.

119
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:49,720
We're now ready to run our column and collect the fractions.

120
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:52,040
Let's open the stopcock.

121
00:07:52,040 --> 00:07:56,440
We don't have to start collecting fractions immediately, as there is no way for our compounds

122
00:07:56,440 --> 00:08:00,960
to immediately be exiting the column, but soon after beginning you will want to begin

123
00:08:00,960 --> 00:08:04,160
collecting fractions in numbered vessels.

124
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:09,520
Typically these will be small Erlenmeyer flasks, but today we will be using test tubes.

125
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:14,160
Start collecting and just move to the next one whenever the current one is full, or sooner

126
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:18,020
if you think that a new substance is about to exit the column.

127
00:08:18,020 --> 00:08:22,720
It is very important that you monitor the solvent level as you collect your samples.

128
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:26,620
When you open the stopcock, the solvent level will start to go down.

129
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:31,260
Remember that we can't let our column dry, so we have to add more solvent as the level

130
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:32,420
goes down.

131
00:08:32,420 --> 00:08:36,980
We also want to be careful when adding more solvent, as we do not want to disturb the

132
00:08:36,980 --> 00:08:37,980
column.

133
00:08:37,980 --> 00:08:42,020
Add more before the level gets all the way down to the gel, and add carefully, letting

134
00:08:42,020 --> 00:08:43,780
it run down the sides.

135
00:08:43,860 --> 00:08:48,940
Continuing, we can see that our mixture is starting to separate into individual components.

136
00:08:48,940 --> 00:08:54,260
Again, since we have colored chemicals, it will be easier to observe the separation.

137
00:08:54,260 --> 00:08:58,960
The dark green mixture we started with is separating into blue and yellow colors.

138
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:01,520
This is why we have to collect fractions.

139
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:06,320
If we just collect these in one container, they will mix together again.

140
00:09:06,320 --> 00:09:10,260
This way we isolate the components in separate fractions.

141
00:09:10,260 --> 00:09:14,720
At first you will be collecting only solvent, but as you can see, the yellow band is getting

142
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:16,480
closer to the bottom.

143
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:21,860
Keep adding the eluent and collecting fractions until you have collected all your compounds.

144
00:09:21,860 --> 00:09:26,460
When the compounds are not visible, you will have to perform TLC to find out what's

145
00:09:26,460 --> 00:09:27,700
in each fraction.

146
00:09:27,700 --> 00:09:32,320
You can test many fractions on one plate, and all the fractions that show data for

147
00:09:32,320 --> 00:09:37,780
a particular compound can be combined and the solvent evaporated, to give you a pure

148
00:09:37,780 --> 00:09:39,520
isolated product.

149
00:09:39,520 --> 00:09:44,020
If you know for sure which compound you want, you can dispose of the rest, but sometimes

150
00:09:44,020 --> 00:09:47,200
you will want to isolate two or more products.

151
00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:52,140
There are times where, rather than TLC, you will want to analyze the fractions by absorbance

152
00:09:52,140 --> 00:09:59,120
spectroscopy, UV, or a colorimetric assay to figure out the identities of any compounds.

153
00:09:59,120 --> 00:10:05,600
But in this particular case we were able to isolate these compounds using just our eyes.

154
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:08,900
Column chromatography can get much more complex than this.

155
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:14,000
In several forms of chromatography, we use more than one solvent system, either in succession

156
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:19,700
or applied as a gradient, usually increasing in polarity over time to push out substances

157
00:10:19,700 --> 00:10:21,420
that traveled more slowly.

158
00:10:21,420 --> 00:10:27,380
Furthermore, the material in the column can be agarose, acrylamide, or some other compound

159
00:10:27,380 --> 00:10:29,460
depending on what we're separating.

160
00:10:29,460 --> 00:10:35,060
In the lab we can use columns to separate things such as peptides or drugs based on

161
00:10:35,060 --> 00:10:40,260
aspects such as size, charge, polarity, or other affinities.

162
00:10:40,260 --> 00:10:45,700
The packing, mixing, and collection of these can also be automated in large-scale academic

163
00:10:45,700 --> 00:10:47,700
and industrial processes.

164
00:10:47,700 --> 00:10:54,660
A sophisticated example of column chromatography is FPLC, or fast protein liquid chromatography,

165
00:10:54,660 --> 00:10:57,740
a technique that is used to separate proteins.

166
00:10:57,740 --> 00:11:13,650
But for now, that concludes an introduction to column chromatography.
